<figcaption>Today's stories by layer: two security items low in the stack need action this week.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Java4Shell puts Java servers at risk of remote code execution</h2>
<p>Researchers at Calif disclosed CVE-2026-114938, dubbed Java4Shell, a flaw in the Base64 decoder of OpenJDK's HotSpot runtime that can write outside its memory when handling malformed input; The Hacker News reports it can enable unauthenticated remote code execution in applications such as Jira, Jenkins and Keycloak<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Updated Java 17, 21, 25 and 27 builds were released on 17 November, and Qualys has published guidance on finding affected runtimes and patching<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Fixes are arriving at different times across Java distributions, and researchers note that turning it into working code execution takes work for each target<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> the hard part is not the patch, it is finding every Java runtime: bundled inside vendor appliances, baked into container base images, pinned in old build pipelines. Start with internet-facing Java services and identity systems such as Keycloak, then rebuild images rather than patching running containers. Ask each vendor whose product ships its own Java when their fixed build lands, and track distributions separately, because Oracle's date is not your distribution's date. A software bill of materials that records the runtime, not just libraries, makes the next one of these a query instead of a hunt.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Microsoft's agent-framework package is poisoned on PyPI</h2>
<p>Versions 1.26.1 and 1.26.2 of Microsoft's agent-framework package were published to PyPI on 17 November with a credential stealer that targeted Azure, Azure DevOps and GitHub Actions secrets<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The code ran through a .pth start-up hook, so it executed whenever Python started on an affected machine, even if the package was never imported; Wiz says 1.26.0 is the latest safe version<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Both releases have been removed.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> treat any machine or CI runner that installed either version as compromised and rotate every secret it could reach, not only the ones the reports name. The structural fix is the same as for every recent package attack: pin versions with hashes, let new releases age before automatic upgrades pick them up, and give CI jobs short-lived, narrowly scoped cloud credentials rather than long-lived secrets in environment variables. Agent frameworks are now core dependencies with broad access, so they deserve the controls you apply to payment code. See <a href="/insights/ai-model-supply-chain-security/">AI model supply chain security</a> and <a href="/insights/vendor-admin-access-rmm-breaches/">vendor admin access as perimeter</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. OpenAI releases GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol</h2>
<p>OpenAI announced GPT-6.2 Astra, aimed at demanding professional work, and GPT-6.2 Sol, aimed at everyday and routine multi-step tasks, available in ChatGPT and Codex for paid plans and through the API<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Coverage reports lower API pricing for Astra and tighter safeguards on higher-risk cybersecurity requests<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> a two-tier release is an invitation to route, not to switch. Run both against your own evaluation set, send routine work to the cheaper tier and keep the larger model for tasks where it measurably wins. If your product relies on security-related prompts, test whether the new safeguards change behaviour before upgrading. See <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">LLM model routing</a> and <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Malaysia's parliament hears the scale of its data centre pipeline</h2>
<p>Malaysia's Dewan Rakyat was told on 18 November that the country's Data Centre Task Force had approved 27 projects worth an estimated RM225.97 billion up to last month, with lawmakers pressing for those investments to bring high-skilled jobs and strengthen AI and cybersecurity capability<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> for companies across Southeast Asia, more local capacity means more choice of region and potentially lower latency, but also more scrutiny of what those facilities deliver locally and who uses their chips. If you plan to host AI workloads in Malaysia, check power and water commitments, export control obligations and local skills expectations alongside price. See the <a href="/case-studies/malaysia-gpu-cloud-export-control-compliance/">Malaysia GPU cloud case study</a> and my <a href="/fractional-cto-singapore/">work in Singapore and Southeast Asia</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Inventory Java runtimes, including those inside vendor products and container images; patch internet-facing services first.</li>
<li>Search lockfiles and CI logs for agent-framework 1.26.1 or 1.26.2; rotate secrets wherever it appears.</li>
<li>Add hash pinning and a release cooling-off period to Python and JavaScript dependency updates.</li>
<li>Evaluate GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol on your own tasks before changing defaults.</li>
<li>If Southeast Asian hosting is on your roadmap, add local policy and export control checks to provider selection.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> and <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. If any of these lands on your desk this week, <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
